(a) The coefficients of $x$ are $3$ and $-1$, so the gradients differ. Both constant terms are $-2$, so the lines have the same $y$-intercept only. They cross the $y$-axis at $(0,-2)$, below the $x$-axis.

The sketch shows their shared intercept.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{lineBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{lineRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}}
\draw[black,<->] (-2.1,0)--(2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2.1)--(0,2.1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[lineBlue,thick,<->] (-0.4,-2)--(0.9333,2);
\draw[lineRed,thick,<->] (-2,1.2)--(1.2,-2);
\fill[black] (0,-0.8) circle (1.5pt);
\path (0,-0.8) node[below left] {$(0,-2)$};
\path (1,1.75) node[right,text=lineBlue] {$y=3x-2$};
\path (-1.5,0.85) node[above left,text=lineRed] {$y=-x-2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(b) One possible equation is $y=2x-2$. Its constant term is also $-2$.
